WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
GPU spend is not only GPU time.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:10.400
Data location, storage type, dataset size, checkpoint strategy, logs, caches, temporary files, snapshots, and transfer patterns all affect cost.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.400
Poor data locality can make an expensive accelerator wait while data moves from somewhere else.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.600
Repeated dataset transfers are a common hidden cost.

5
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:29.600
If every run copies the same large dataset to a new location, the team may pay in both transfer cost and lost productive GPU time.

6
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.400
Better placement, staging, or caching can help when it is designed intentionally.

7
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.400
Checkpoints and artifacts are valuable, but they need retention rules.

8
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.600
During active training, checkpoints support recovery and comparison.

9
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:50.400
After a decision is made, the team should know which files must be kept, which can be compacted, and which should expire.

10
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.800
Without rules, old experiments can turn into long-term storage cost.

11
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.400
Caches can save money when they reduce repeated work.

12
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.600
They can also become waste if nobody owns them or measures their value.

13
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.000
A useful cache has a purpose, a lifetime, and monitoring that shows whether it still helps.

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.800
Logs and metrics need the same discipline.

15
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.400
They should support troubleshooting, cost review, and operational accountability without becoming uncontrolled storage growth.

16
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.800
Hidden costs become manageable when data movement and retention are treated as part of workload design.

17
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:34.000
A good review asks where data lives before the job starts, where outputs go after it finishes, and which files are worth keeping.

18
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.600
Those questions often reveal avoidable transfers and stale artifacts.
